{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1171"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge society/business","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/society__business.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",38],["+","north, "],["=",772],["+","Sweden belongs on the list in its own way. It has been obliged to adopt the euro since joining the EU in 1995, but its voters rejected it in a referendum in 2003 and the country keeps the krona – while its people abandon cash altogether. By 2016 only about two per cent of the value of payments in Sweden was made in cash – shoppers paid by card or with the mobile app Swish – and many shops, buses and even churches no longer accept notes and coins.\n\n"],["=",123],["+","\n| Sweden | Swedish krona | SEK | 10.3 |"],["=",40]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",28],["+","im Norden, "],["=",762],["+","Schweden gehört auf seine eigene Weise auf die Liste. Seit seinem EU-Beitritt 1995 ist es zur Einführung des Euro verpflichtet, doch seine Wähler lehnten ihn 2003 in einer Volksabstimmung ab, und das Land behält die Krone – während seine Bürger das Bargeld ganz aufgeben. 2016 wurden wertmäßig nur noch rund zwei Prozent der Zahlungen in Schweden bar geleistet – man zahlte per Karte oder mit der Handy-App Swish – und viele Geschäfte, Busse und sogar Kirchen nehmen keine Scheine und Münzen mehr an.\n\n"],["=",121],["+","\n| Schweden | Schwedische Krone | SEK | 10,3 |"],["=",36]]}]},
